    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec requires every 400/409/429 rejection to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body carrying `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. The existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper already produced a fully-populated `ProblemDetail` (type `about:blank`, title = exception name, status, detail), but nine handlers still returned plain `String`/`Map` bodies and OfficeFloor's default responder emitted `application/json` even for `ProblemDetail` (confirmed: the pre-change response was `application/json` with body `{\"errors\":[\"city\"]}`).\n\nTo keep structural impact low I added **one small cohesive helper** rather than growing any handler:\n\n- `ProblemDetails.respond(ProblemDetail)` \u2014 wraps a detail as a `ResponseEntity` with `Content-Type: application/problem+json` and the detail's status. This is the only place the content-type rule lives.\n\nThen each affected handler became a single delegating line (CC 1, no surrounding context to hold):\n\n- **400**: `DisposableEmailDomain`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidPostcode`, `MissingOwnerFields` (was a `Map`), and `ValidationExceptionHandler` (already built a `ProblemDetail`, just re-routed its send through the helper so it gets the problem+json content type).\n- **409**: `CrowdedCity`, `DuplicateHousehold`, `DuplicateOwnerEmail`, `DuplicateOwnerTelephone`.\n- **429**: `DailyLimit`.\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged. Handlers outside the 400/409/429 scope (404 `NotFound`, 403 `Forbidden`, 500 `General`, `DataIntegrityViolation`) were left untouched.\n\nFull suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 including the `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` acceptance test asserting `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`.",
